\section{Introduction}
Eigen starts from a question about description: which distinctions must a computational system preserve to do useful work? A smaller representation is not necessarily a better one. It must still answer the question, predict the relevant change or reconstruct the information on which an action depends. The proposed programme is to make that requirement explicit, account for its cost, and use the failure of an inadequate description to decide what to retain next.

Two tasks sharing one register provide a simple entry point. Sharing saves a resource, but the tasks may prefer different states. Their compromise has a price. A summary of an evolving process faces a related choice: retain another variable, retain some history, or accept a specified prediction error. A new open-systems calculation makes the strategy especially concrete: absorb a spin's coupling to its surrounding oscillators exactly, then predict its low-temperature state through a controlled correction. In the quantum-computing examples the practical question is which part of a large state or spectrum deserves computational effort. In memory it is which relations among source records must survive compression. These are the connections the account develops, with the maps and assumptions needed to make each useful.

Eigen's contribution is a synthesis and a method of construction: familiar mathematics used to choose, test and refine useful representations. The supporting papers provide different parts of it. The prototypes supply particular calculations and comparisons. The Writing App provides a present engineering system in which persistent objects, evidence and authorised changes can be inspected together. A standalone extensible software development kit (SDK) is the proposed next product, with an operating environment for structured knowledge and agent work as the longer-term direction.

The criterion for judging this programme is practical: does choosing and refining the representation improve the answer at an honestly counted cost? The following account gives that question enough mathematical and implementation detail to support a decision, without assuming quantum background. Sources and archived evidence are linked at the points where they bear on the argument.\footnote{\href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=development\%2Fhmf-integrated-edition-inventory-20261016.json&expected_sha256=501f17597c6fb5070e11fb48c2cd38541fc1044ee5ae8b75ed624425542dbb8a&owner=writing-artifact&artifact_id=ART-a89da540f1a54718b31673165c74f064}{Exact paper editions, project homes and PDF inventory}. The linked reading notes retain derivation passages, source code and archived result routes, with their qualifications. \href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=development\%2Feditorial-weld-detail-register-20261016.json&expected_sha256=ea1f56c4002ffa860493725e8e7f1002dd17cc5705b3f2b932a039e580d08290&owner=writing-artifact&artifact_id=ART-18c3dd3bc14c46f6880df05a9defe7a3}{Detailed numerical comparisons and provenance} remain available alongside the figures.}

\paragraph{A route through the evidence.}
The questions in Tom's review and Jack's revised scope have direct answers:
\begin{description}
\item[Core claim.] A useful representation preserves task-relevant distinctions at an explicit cost. Sections~\ref{section:the-price-of-a-shared-description}--\ref{section:loss-dynamics-and-the-choice-of-scale} develop the shared-register calculation, retained dynamics and loss geometry from the named papers.
\item[Closest calculations.] Section~\ref{section:loss-dynamics-and-the-choice-of-scale} derives the bath-coupled ground-state prediction and compares it with independent diagonalisation in Fig.~\ref{fig:dd-hmf-physical-ground-state-20261016}. Section~\ref{section:quantum-prototypes-changing-the-calculation} connects the loss-operator and residual methods to their code and archived comparisons. Operator diagnostics explain weak feedback, while gap-aware enlargement improves a deliberately structured example. These motivate matched-cost tests on less favourable problems.
\item[Useful applications and alternatives.] Section~\ref{section:memory-as-a-retained-and-reconstructable-object} follows both Corpus as a State and Memory in Pieces into reconstruction and joint queries. Its comparisons distinguish exact relational computation from language retrieval, and place GraphRAG, RAPTOR and retrieval-centric learning alongside the proposed method.
\item[Implementation and judgement.] The Writing App is the integrated backend available to inspect now. Section~\ref{section:the-backend-objects-authority-and-revision} follows a guarded change and explains who judges claim support. Start in the repository at \nolinkurl{docs/writing-source-map.md}.
\item[Evidence of development and next decision.] Section~\ref{section:research-that-can-inspect-its-own-results} separates the original SuperARC benchmark from Eigen's campaign and its later corrections. Sections~\ref{section:from-a-working-core-to-an-extensible-sdk}--\ref{section:the-collection-and-the-next-tests} specify an SDK extension test and the comparisons that would most usefully establish value.
\end{description}

\section{The price of a shared description}
\label{section:the-price-of-a-shared-description}

Suppose two computations use the same stored parameter $q\in\mathbb R^2$. One prefers $a=(1,0)$ and the other prefers $b=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$. Their losses are
\begin{equation}
 \ell_1(q)=\tfrac12\|q-a\|^2,\qquad
 \ell_2(q)=\tfrac12\|q-b\|^2,\qquad
 d^2=\|a-b\|^2=2(1-\cos\theta).
 \label{eq:register-losses}
\end{equation}
We take $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$. Here $\theta$ measures disagreement between the tasks and $d$ is the distance between their preferred settings. Neither task is difficult on its own. The difficulty comes from asking one register to serve both.

Give the first task weight $\alpha$ and the second weight $1-\alpha$, with $0\leq\alpha\leq1$. Completing the square gives
\begin{equation}
 \alpha\ell_1+(1-\alpha)\ell_2
 =\tfrac12\|q-q_*\|^2+\tfrac12\alpha(1-\alpha)d^2,
 \qquad q_*=\alpha a+(1-\alpha)b.
 \label{eq:register-square}
\end{equation}
The first term can be removed by optimisation. The second cannot: it is the price of the compromise within this representation. At equal weights it is $d^2/8$. If maintaining a second register costs an additional $c$, separate descriptions are preferable when $c<d^2/8$. The relevant comparison is between the total costs of two ways of performing the same pair of tasks, not between their storage sizes in isolation.

\emph{Self-Consistent Loss Geometries}\footnote{\href{/projection/pdf?corpus=self-consistent-loss-geometries-coupled-evaluators&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fself-consistent-loss-geometries-coupled-evaluators\%2Fmanuscript\%2Fimported\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3A09d4a6fbba973396e19440d042a374b2dd5d5eeda73a1c4619d904ead2644f46}{Self-Consistent Loss Geometries and the Laws of Learning (PDF)}, Section IX.A, supplies the coupled-evaluator toy. The distinct \href{/projection/pdf?corpus=self-consistent-loss-geometries&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fself-consistent-loss-geometries\%2Fmanuscript\%2Fimported\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3Abccdb2efd5c12ef9c837f63e6ffa92c83a4885c0b4a9cbc6c331457ff1d67d27}{Self-Consistent Loss Geometries and Resolution-Stable Learning (PDF)} develops the PRX-format variant.~\cite{McCaulb0d064}}~\cite{McCaulda239a} develops a richer version in which the system can also change the attention paid to each task. It adds a conflict charge $\gamma\alpha(1-\alpha)d^2$ and an arbitration penalty $2\eta(\alpha-\tfrac12)^2$. The parameters $\gamma\geq0$ and $\eta>0$ specify those modelled costs. The second penalty makes ignoring a task costly. After optimising $q$ and $\alpha$, the fixed shared-register model has
\begin{equation}
 L_B^*=\min\left\{\frac{d^2}{4}\left(\tfrac12+\gamma\right),
                         \frac{\eta}{2}\right\}.
 \label{eq:shared-optimum}
\end{equation}
Its options are balanced compromise or suppression of one evaluator. In the split option each task has its own minimising coordinate, removing the disagreement penalty by construction. The remaining incremental partition cost is $C_{\rm part}\geq0$, so the system achieves
\begin{equation}
 L_C^*=\min\{L_B^*,C_{\rm part}\}.
 \label{eq:partition-optimum}
\end{equation}
Figure~\ref{fig:dd-shared-register-price} makes the choice visible. At the stated register price, splitting becomes favourable before the fixed shared representation would suppress an evaluator. The useful adaptation is a change in what the register distinguishes.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/portable-run-b69220b3a27348ad.result/dd-shared-register-price.pdf}
\caption{When should two tasks stop sharing a register? (a) The equally weighted quadratic compromise is $q_*=(a+b)/2$. (b) Fixed-register loss $L_B^*$ and partition-enabled loss $L_C^*$ versus disagreement angle $\theta$, for $\gamma=1$, $\eta=0.8$ and $C_{\rm part}=0.25$. Splitting becomes cheaper at $\theta=0.8411$ radians, before the fixed-register model suppresses an evaluator at $1.0853$. The curves evaluate the classical cost model in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:shared-optimum}--\eqref{eq:partition-optimum}.}
\label{fig:dd-shared-register-price}
\end{figure}

The classical model turns representation choice into an ordinary optimisation: tolerate the compromise or pay to remove it. The same question will recur in the quantum and memory examples: when should additional capacity buy a new distinction rather than more work inside the existing description?\footnote{\href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=reading\%2Fshared-register-and-prx-interpretation-20261015.md&expected_sha256=77b50c6d0c9899d205f66afd614e127f162cb9f915af1239ad5d4a0a64f83299&owner=writing-artifact}{Shared-register derivation, exact editions and numerical producer}. The original edition emphasises coupled evaluators, while the PRX variant studies resolution-stable update laws. Neither selected edition supplies the further derivation needed for the recalled quantum uncertainty inequality.}
\paragraph{A phase boundary for sharing.}
Varying the register price as well as the disagreement reveals three regimes, shown in Figure~\ref{fig:dd-register-phase-diagram}. The boundary follows directly from the same objective. Write $A(\theta)=(\tfrac12+\gamma)d^2$. After minimising the shared coordinate, its remaining attention cost is
\[
 \frac{A(\theta)}4+\bigl(2\eta-A(\theta)\bigr)
                   \left(\alpha-\tfrac12\right)^2.
\]
Balanced sharing is therefore optimal when $A<2\eta$, while the shared model suppresses one evaluator when $A>2\eta$. Allowing the register itself to split gives the short result
\begin{equation}
 L_*=\min\left\{C_{\rm part},\frac{A(\theta)}4,\frac{\eta}2\right\},
 \qquad
 C_{\rm crit}(\theta)=
 \min\left\{\frac{\tfrac12+\gamma}{2}(1-\cos\theta),\frac{\eta}2\right\}.
 \label{eq:register-phase-boundary}
\end{equation}
For $C_{\rm part}<C_{\rm crit}$, two registers are preferable. Above the boundary, one is cheaper. Equality gives tied representations. The optimal cost is continuous across the share--split boundary, but generically has a kink as the preferred number of registers changes. This is an optimisation phase transition in a finite model.

At $A=2\eta$, every shared attention weight is degenerate. This boundary is reachable for $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$ when $\eta\leq2(\tfrac12+\gamma)$. The flat part of the boundary is informative. Once conflict is sufficiently large, the shared model can pay $\eta/2$ to abandon an evaluator instead of buying a second register. With $\gamma=1$ and $\eta=0.8$, the three regions meet at $\theta_{\rm att}=\arccos(1-\eta/(\tfrac12+\gamma))\simeq1.0853$ and $C_{\rm part}=0.4$. The earlier $C_{\rm part}=0.25$ example is a horizontal slice: increasing disagreement crosses from balanced sharing to separate registers at $\theta_{\rm split}\simeq0.8411$. The diagram makes the design choice explicit - tolerate a compromise, pay for another distinction, or sacrifice one of the tasks.\footnote{\href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=development\%2Fregister-phase-boundary-20261015.md&expected_sha256=10bd988062642312606a42b006bb2a810f95abbc64e7a057d6671319292be444&owner=writing-artifact&artifact_id=ART-c852a2939ab04e109d5325fd3aa381db}{Algebraic derivation, boundary conditions and reproducible phase-diagram checks}.}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.93\linewidth]{figures/phase-run-939b6a8b76724d02.result/dd-register-phase-diagram.pdf}
\caption{The register-choice phase diagram for $\gamma=1$ and $\eta=0.8$. Below $C_{\rm crit}(\theta)$, splitting retains both tasks. Above it, the shared optimum is balanced at small $\theta$ and suppresses an evaluator at large $\theta$. Solid boundaries mark tied costs, including the vertical attention boundary above the meeting point. At that meeting point all shared attention weights are degenerate and splitting has the same cost. The dashed $C_{\rm part}=0.25$ slice recovers Figure~\ref{fig:dd-shared-register-price}. The regions are the exact optima of the declared finite model.}
\label{fig:dd-register-phase-diagram}
\end{figure}
\FloatBarrier

The register example assumes a price for an extra description. A separate question is how to choose a cost coordinate when independent capacities compose. \emph{The Cost of Complexity}\footnote{\href{/projection/pdf?corpus=cost-of-complexity&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fcost-of-complexity\%2Fmanuscript\%2Ftex\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3Ab1f6599d1c78ffbc2e7ffe07d003b5223aac58a26850e8a953cb804611875983}{The Cost of Complexity (PDF)} and \href{/projection/pdf?corpus=synthetics-foundations&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fsynthetics-foundations\%2Fmanuscript\%2FSynthetics-Foundations.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3A7be6d22efea797d5907b03f42086cb90e216adb5422d327a16bc012b84389dfa}{Foundations of Synthetics (PDF)}, Chapter 1, Sections 1.2--1.6: compositional capacity, reset protocols, cost response, prediction and selective refinement.} and the current \emph{Foundations of Synthetics} motivate a logarithmic coordinate from multiplicative capacity and additive cost.~\cite{Reference975536} If independent capacities $M$ and $N$ compose multiplicatively, while their costs add, a nondecreasing cost function obeying $f(MN)=f(M)+f(N)$ has the form $f(N)=\kappa\log N$. The scale $\kappa\geq0$ fixes the cost unit. The premise concerns the capacity or product-order count being composed. A single globally sorted probability rank is a different object and need not multiply. This distinction matters: forcing two partially ordered descriptions into one global list can introduce comparisons that neither task required.

For fixed labels $r=1,\ldots,R$ with cost $\log r$, maximum entropy chooses the least concentrated probability law compatible with a specified mean cost. The partition sum below normalises those probabilities. The resulting family is
\begin{equation}
 p_\lambda(r)=\frac{r^{-\lambda}}{Z_R(\lambda)},\qquad
 Z_R(\lambda)=\sum_{r=1}^{R}r^{-\lambda}.
 \label{eq:cost-family}
\end{equation}
The multiplier $\lambda$ is the price conjugate to mean log-cost. Increasing it concentrates probability on cheaper labels. If $C_R=\sum_r p_\lambda(r)\log r$, then
\begin{equation}
 C_R=-\partial_\lambda\log Z_R,\qquad
 -\frac{dC_R}{d\lambda}
 =\operatorname{Var}_{p_\lambda}(\log r)
 =g_{\lambda\lambda}.
 \label{eq:cost-geometry}
\end{equation}
The response coefficient measures how distinguishable nearby probability laws are. It is also the Fisher information of this same one-parameter family, obtained by averaging $(\partial_\lambda\log p_\lambda)^2$. Thus cost response and statistical distinguishability are two readings of one construction. For a small change $\delta\lambda$, the relative entropy is
$D(p_\lambda\|p_{\lambda+\delta\lambda})
=\tfrac12g_{\lambda\lambda}(\delta\lambda)^2+O((\delta\lambda)^3)$.
This is a specific bridge from a cost coordinate to geometry, with the underlying probability distribution held in view. Calibrating $C_{\rm part}$ in seconds, joules or bytes is a further application-level task: the representational price must be connected to the resources and prediction error that matter.

At finite $R$ these expressions are smooth. The infinite counting-measure model has $Z_\infty(\lambda)=\zeta(\lambda)$ only for $\lambda>1$, and becomes singular at its normalisability boundary. This distinguishes a singular infinite limit from the smooth finite models. Physical heat, in turn, is charged to an operation on an ensemble. Landauer's reset analysis concerns the effect of a specified erasure protocol on information-bearing physical states.\footnote{R. Landauer, \emph{Irreversibility and Heat Generation in the Computing Process}, IBM Journal of Research and Development 5 (1961), \href{https://doi.org/10.1147/rd.53.0183}{doi:10.1147/rd.53.0183}.} Foundations makes this distinction concrete using retained side information and explicit bath/register operations.~\cite{Landauer1961f2ecf4}

A task supplies the other half of the accounting. Let $\Omega$ denote the underlying situation, $X$ its retained description and $Y$ the quantity to be predicted. For an encoder with $Y$ conditionally independent of $X$ given $\Omega$, and a decoder distribution $q(Y|X)$, the expected logarithmic prediction loss separates as
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[-\log q(Y|X)]
 =H(Y|\Omega)+I(Y;\Omega|X)
   +\mathbb E_X D\!\left(P(Y|X)\|q(Y|X)\right).
 \label{eq:predictive-loss}
\end{equation}
Here $H$ is the uncertainty left after the stated condition is known. The conditional mutual information $I(Y;\Omega|X)$ measures the predictive information about $Y$ available in $\Omega$ but absent from $X$. Relative entropy is $D(p\Vert q)=\sum_y p(y)\log[p(y)/q(y)]$: the excess expected logarithmic prediction loss from using $q$ when the outcomes follow $p$. The terms are intrinsic target uncertainty, distinctions lost by the encoder, and mismatch in the decoder. Better decoding cannot recover a distinction that was discarded. A larger description cannot remove randomness already present in the target.

Foundations illustrates this with three input bits $(a,b,c)$. The bit $c$ labels a sensor but does not affect the alarm probability. For $(a,b)=(00,01,10,11)$ those probabilities are $0.1,0.3,0.7,0.9$. Retaining $(a,b)$ preserves the forecasts exactly while discarding $c$. Retaining only $a$ gives the coarser forecasts $0.2$ and $0.8$ under the declared uniform input law. The adequate representation depends on the prediction being requested, not on preserving every input bit.

The next distinction can also have a local value. With a false stop costing one unit and a missed alarm three, the optimal alarm threshold is probability $1/4$. After observing $a=0$, acquiring $b$ reduces conditional decision risk from $0.6$ to $0.5$. After $a=1$, it does not change the preferred action. At acquisition cost $k<0.1$, buying $b$ only on the first branch improves total expected cost from $0.40$ to $0.35+0.5k$. Uniformly increasing resolution would pay for information where it changes nothing.\footnote{\href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=reading\%2Fcost-and-foundations-ch1-interpretation-20261015.md&expected_sha256=77e57c86160788fc0e65c89d58258885246e747a824e80f69a8c70e2f5737f60&owner=writing-artifact}{Cost, predictive decomposition, selective acquisition and current Foundations source map}.}

This also explains why a purely greedy refinement rule can be inadequate. A local step may cost more than its immediate benefit but unlock a valuable later calculation. For a finite refinement tree, an incremental gain $a_v$ at node $v$ and conditional child probabilities $q(u|v)$ give the backward recursion
\begin{equation}
 V(v)=\max\left\{0,\ a_v+\sum_u q(u|v)V(u)\right\}.
 \label{eq:refinement-value}
\end{equation}
A step with immediate gain $-1$ can be worthwhile if it unlocks gain $5$ with probability $0.6$: its continuation value is $2$. Efficient representation is a policy for purchasing the distinctions and intermediate constructions that make useful work possible. When the task is to predict an evolving process, the value of a distinction depends on what it lets us say about the future. A summary that answers today's question may not contain enough information to answer tomorrow's.

\section{What a useful summary must remember}
\label{section:what-a-useful-summary-must-remember}

Imagine a process that moves among four states.\footnote{\href{/projection/pdf?corpus=synthetics-foundations&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fsynthetics-foundations\%2Fmanuscript\%2FSynthetics-Foundations.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3A7be6d22efea797d5907b03f42086cb90e216adb5422d327a16bc012b84389dfa}{Foundations of Synthetics (PDF)}, Chapter 3, Sections 3.3--3.6: coarse generators, finite-horizon error, selection of a useful partition and exact memory. The four-state example below makes those maps explicit.} States 1 and 2 belong to region A, and states 3 and 4 to region B. Motion within either region is fast. There are also two routes between the regions, one slow and one three times faster. A reader interested only in the probability of being in A might reasonably try to replace the four states by two.

That summary loses something immediately. Starting in state 1 or state 2 gives the same retained probability, $p_A(0)=1$, but different initial escape rates: $p_A'(0)=-1$ or $-3$ in the chosen time units. No autonomous two-state law can assign both derivatives to the same initial state. Figure~\ref{fig:dd-retention-memory} shows the missing distinction and two ways to retain its effect.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/portable-run-b69220b3a27348ad.result/dd-retention-memory.pdf}
\caption{What the coarse state omits. Four states exchange probability at the labelled symmetric rates. Starting in state 1 or 2 gives $p_A(0)=1$, but initial rates $-1$ and $-3$. Retaining $z=(p_1-p_2)-(p_3-p_4)$ closes the prediction for $y=p_A-p_B$, with $\dot y=-4y+2z$ and $\dot z=2y-12z$. Eliminating $z$ gives an exact memory term instead. This constructed example makes the retention and residual mechanisms in Foundations and Memory in Pieces explicit.}
\label{fig:dd-retention-memory}
\end{figure}

The example uses a column probability vector $p=(p_1,p_2,p_3,p_4)^T$ and the generator
\begin{equation}
 \dot p=Gp,\qquad
 G=\begin{pmatrix}
 -5&4&1&0\\
 4&-7&0&3\\
 1&0&-5&4\\
 0&3&4&-7
 \end{pmatrix},\qquad
 C=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&0&0\\0&0&1&1\end{pmatrix}.
 \label{eq:four-state}
\end{equation}
Off-diagonal entries of $G$ are transition rates and each column sums to zero, preserving total probability. The rectangular matrix $C$ retains $(p_A,p_B)^T=Cp$. It is an explicitly many-to-one map between two spaces of probability distributions.

A reconstruction map specifies what the reduced description stands for. Given a strictly positive reference distribution $\pi$, the conditional reconstruction associated with this partition is
\begin{equation}
 R=D_\pi C^T D_{C\pi}^{-1},\qquad CR=I,\qquad B=CGR,
 \label{eq:reconstruction}
\end{equation}
where $D_v$ is the diagonal matrix with vector $v$ on its diagonal. Within each coarse cell, $R$ restores the relative weights supplied by $\pi$. Here the stationary distribution is uniform, so $R=C^T/2$ and
$B=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}-2&2\\2&-2\end{smallmatrix}\right)$.
The reduced law predicts the average escape rate, not either hidden starting state separately.

The discrepancy between evolving first and reducing first is the closure residual
\begin{equation}
 \Delta=CG-BC.
 \label{eq:closure-residual}
\end{equation}
It maps a fine distribution to the difference between two proposed coarse derivatives. If $\Delta=0$, the retained state evolves autonomously for every fine initial distribution. For a partition of a Markov chain, this requires all fine states in a coarse cell to have the same total exit rate into each other coarse cell. The usual name is strong lumpability. Matching an equilibrium distribution is much weaker: with the stationary fit in Eq.~\eqref{eq:reconstruction}, $\Delta\pi=0$ even when the other states do not close.

The residual has a quantitative use beyond detecting exact failure. For finite Markov generators,
\begin{equation}
 C e^{tG}-e^{tB}C
 =\int_0^t e^{(t-s)B}\Delta e^{sG}\,ds.
 \label{eq:duhamel}
\end{equation}
Because stochastic evolution contracts the $\ell^1$ norm, a probability vector satisfies
$\|(C e^{tG}-e^{tB}C)p\|_1\leq\min\{2,t\|\Delta\|_1\}$.
Here $\|\Delta\|_1$ is the induced matrix $1$-norm. The horizon $t$ matters. A small instantaneous discrepancy may accumulate, and a bound useful for a short prediction need not justify indefinitely reusing the same reduced law.

For the four-state process, a particularly economical repair is to retain one imbalance,
\[
 y=p_1+p_2-p_3-p_4,\qquad
 z=p_1-p_2-p_3+p_4.
\]
Direct multiplication of Eq.~\eqref{eq:four-state} gives
\begin{equation}
 \dot y=-4y+2z,\qquad \dot z=2y-12z.
 \label{eq:two-coordinate}
\end{equation}
These two coordinates close exactly for the desired task, predicting $p_A=(1+y)/2$ while leaving other microstate distinctions unresolved.

Alternatively, solve the second equation for $z$ and substitute it into the first:
\begin{equation}
 \dot y(t)=-4y(t)+2e^{-12t}z(0)
       +4\int_0^t e^{-12(t-s)}y(s)\,ds.
 \label{eq:four-state-memory}
\end{equation}
The extra variable has become a memory kernel and an initial-condition term. The discarded coordinate's influence is retained through the history. Figure~\ref{fig:dd-retention-trajectories} compares the exact trajectories with the two-state approximation that omits this information.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/portable-run-b69220b3a27348ad.result/dd-retention-trajectories.pdf}
\caption{Equal summaries, different futures. Exact region probabilities $p_A(t)$ for the four-state process, starting in state 1 or 2. The coarse model without the hidden imbalance $z$ predicts the same curve for both initial states. It initially lies between the exact trajectories but does not remain between them. Retaining $z$, or its exact memory kernel, recovers both trajectories. Time $t$ is in inverse base-rate units. The curves are analytic trajectories of Eq.~\eqref{eq:four-state}.}
\label{fig:dd-retention-trajectories}
\end{figure}

This is the elementary form of an established projection method. For a linear evolution $\dot x=Lx$, a projection $P$ and $Q=I-P$, write $u=Px$ and $w=Qx$. Exact elimination of $w$ gives
\begin{align}
 \dot u(t)={}&PLP\,u(t)+PLQ\,e^{tQLQ}w(0)\nonumber\\
 &+\int_0^t PLQ\,e^{(t-s)QLQ}QLP\,u(s)\,ds.
 \label{eq:general-memory}
\end{align}
Mori's original treatment makes clear why choosing the retained variables is decisive: a slow mode left in the discarded sector can invalidate a supposed short-memory approximation.\footnote{H. Mori, \emph{Transport, Collective Motion, and Brownian Motion}, Progress of Theoretical Physics 33 (1965),423--455, \href{https://doi.org/10.1143/ptp.33.423}{doi:10.1143/ptp.33.423}.} A local-in-time replacement needs a separation of scales or another explicit error control. Elimination alone supplies neither dissipation nor thermalisation.~\cite{Mori19656369be}

Hierarchy becomes useful when these accounts compose. If $C_{01}$ reduces level 0 to level 1 and $C_{12}$ reduces level 1 to level 2, their defects obey
\begin{equation}
 \Delta_{02}=C_{12}\Delta_{01}+\Delta_{12}C_{01}.
 \label{eq:composed-residual}
\end{equation}
The coarse model inherits the earlier mismatch as well as adding its own. A parent cannot declare itself accurate merely because it fits the already truncated child. This is the mathematical reason to transport an error account with a retained object.

Closure must still serve a task. The current Foundations chapter enumerates all 2,047 bipartitions of a twelve-state model with three motifs. Its exactly closed hub/spoke partition retains essentially none of the motif-label information the task asks for. Imposing a retention requirement instead exposes a useful trade-off between predictive closure and the distinctions being preserved. The source also finds that an appropriate sign cut of the slow eigenfunction selects a good partition. The task determines which spectral or closure criterion is useful.\footnote{\href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=reading\%2Ffoundations-ch2-ch3-interpretation-20261015.md&expected_sha256=6340f47b950080e85681d9bd0f8ce0f8d2adf1eb79981078490d3c02f91b25df&owner=writing-artifact}{Current Foundations derivations, finite examples and figure-source interpretation}.}

The four-state example asks whether a summary can carry a prediction forward in time. A different test is whether several summaries can be combined into one consistent account. Here the information at risk is which observations belong together. A \emph{context} is a set of measurements that can be made jointly. If an observable belongs to two contexts, its identity must remain the same in both. Foundations Chapter 2 asks whether the joint probability tables for overlapping contexts can all be obtained as marginals of one context-independent distribution.~\cite{McCaul85986c}

Its two-qubit Peres--Mermin example makes the obstruction elementary. Arrange nine observables in a square. Each has outcomes $+1$ or $-1$, and every row and column is a jointly measurable context. The operator products require
\[
 \begin{array}{c|ccc}
 \text{context}&1&2&3\\ \hline
 \text{row product}&+1&+1&+1\\
 \text{column product}&+1&+1&-1
 \end{array}.
\]
Suppose one value could be assigned to every cell independently of how it was measured. Multiplying all six context products would use each cell twice, giving $+1$. The required signs instead multiply to $-1$. At least one context constraint must fail. If $W$ counts agreement with each required sign as $+1$ and disagreement as $-1$, every such assignment and every probability mixture obeys $W\le4$, while the quantum product identities give $W=6$. The source specifies the nine operators and the complete outcome tables. The crucial retained information is the overlap of measurement identities, not merely six unrelated summaries.

This is different from the dynamical closure residual: one tests a common probability account, the other tests autonomous evolution of a retained state. A change of coordinates or frame can change the matrices used to describe the same observables without changing their predictions. That freedom of representation must be distinguished from changing a measurement context, which changes the jointly accessible questions. The spectral-transport construction in the next section makes one such frame comparison explicit. Together these connect retained distinctions, overlapping measurement contexts and changes of spectral frame.

The constructive common theme is fidelity under a specified operation. A useful summary preserves a forecast or decision. A useful reduced dynamical state carries enough information to predict its evolution. A useful hierarchy carries the inherited cost of what it discarded. These requirements give the next representation a job, rather than treating increasing abstraction as an achievement in itself.

\section{Loss, dynamics and the choice of scale}
\label{section:loss-dynamics-and-the-choice-of-scale}

To decide which distinctions are worth retaining, we also need to say what counts as an error. A loss function says which answers we prefer. It does not yet say how a system moves between them. The distinction matters because two procedures can have the same optimum and radically different routes towards it. A probability distribution over possible configurations gives one way to describe both uncertainty and exploration. Relative entropy then prices departure from a reference distribution, while a generator specifies the actual motion.

The Law of Laws\footnote{\href{/projection/pdf?corpus=law-of-laws&file=synthetics+papers\%2Flaw-of-laws\%2Fmanuscript\%2Ftex\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3Abcbf6d780a9caab9fcda91f87f0823879e3fd6c2d478f29cc616a1409d5dd5d5}{The Law of Laws (PDF)} and the consequential \href{/projection/pdf?corpus=synthetics-statistical-lawhood&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fsynthetics-statistical-lawhood\%2Fmanuscript\%2Fimported\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3A1c2768dac0c823c20bbca50b04338f0c20fadd8b3dd0658b57d2d892b75c6c7d}{Synthetics: Statistical Lawhood Under Finite Description (PDF)} edition. The calculations here use the explicit conditions developed in \href{/projection/pdf?corpus=synthetics-foundations&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fsynthetics-foundations\%2Fmanuscript\%2FSynthetics-Foundations.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3A7be6d22efea797d5907b03f42086cb90e216adb5422d327a16bc012b84389dfa}{Foundations of Synthetics (PDF)}, Chapter 3.} develops this combination in the functional
\begin{equation}
 {\cal F}[p]=\sum_x p(x)K(x)+M\sum_x p(x)\log\frac{p(x)}{q(x)},
 \qquad
 p_*(x)=\frac{q(x)e^{-K(x)/M}}{Z}.
 \label{eq:law-functional}
\end{equation}
Here $p$ is a normalised distribution, $q$ a positive normalised reference, $K$ the cost of a configuration and $M>0$ the price of concentrating probability away from $q$. The normalising constant is $Z=\sum_xq(x)e^{-K(x)/M}$. Substitution gives the exact identity
\[
 {\cal F}[p]-{\cal F}[p_*]=M D_{\rm KL}(p\Vert p_*),
 \qquad {\cal F}[p_*]=-M\log Z.
\]
The preferred distribution is therefore fixed by an explicit competition between cost and concentration.~\cite{Reference0e02fc} The relative entropy on the right measures the excess objective.

This construction survives elimination of hidden variables in a useful sense. Write the configuration as $(y,\xi)$, with retained coordinate $y$ and discarded coordinate $\xi$. Summing over $\xi$ gives the effective cost
\begin{equation}
 K_{\rm eff}(y)
 =-M\log\sum_\xi q(\xi|y)e^{-K(y,\xi)/M}.
 \label{eq:effective-law}
\end{equation}
The marginal distribution has the same form as Eq.~\eqref{eq:law-functional}, now with reference $q(y)$ and cost $K_{\rm eff}(y)$. A description can remain in one mathematical family while its parameters change with resolution. For the source's elementary Ising example, each spin is a sign $\sigma_i=\pm1$ and a neighbouring pair has energy $-J\sigma_i\sigma_{i+1}$. Eliminating a middle spin means summing its two possible signs. For retained neighbours $a,c=\pm1$, the factor is $\sum_{u=\pm1}e^{Ju(a+c)/M}=2\cosh[J(a+c)/M]$. Matching the factors for $a=c$ and $a=-c$ changes the nearest-neighbour coupling to $J'=(M/2)\log\cosh(2J/M)$. The coordinate $x=\tanh(J/M)$ obeys the simple map $x'=x^2$. This is a concrete instance of the renormalisation idea: follow how an effective description changes, rather than insisting that its original parameters survive intact.

A stationary distribution does not specify a clock. Different generators can preserve the same distribution, and non-reversible ones can circulate probability around it. The equilibrium elimination in Eq.~\eqref{eq:effective-law} consequently does not establish the dynamical closure in Eq.~\eqref{eq:closure-residual}. The two results complement one another. One constructs an effective cost. The other tests whether the chosen retained state has enough information to evolve autonomously.\footnote{\href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=reading\%2Flawhood-editions-interpretation-20261015.md&expected_sha256=f94f5a2834fb0d7e418cf660c4dc2413b2f566bded1be7b9d381e9b366937fed&owner=writing-artifact}{Law of Laws editions, conditional elimination and the distinction between stationary law and kinetics}.}

\paragraph{A subsystem carries the influence of its surroundings.}
Eliminating variables becomes a physical question when they describe an interacting environment. A qubit in a device is not an isolated two-state object. It interacts with nearby modes, control circuitry and other degrees of freedom. Those interactions can change both its equilibrium probabilities and the information needed to predict its motion. Drawing a boundary around the qubit does not switch the interactions off. It changes what we must encode on the inside if we no longer describe the outside explicitly. A qubit has two basis states. We write a state column vector as $|\psi\rangle$ and its conjugate transpose as $\langle\psi|$, so $\langle\psi|O|\psi\rangle$ is ordinary matrix multiplication giving the mean measured value of an observable $O$. Observables are represented by Hermitian matrices, equal to their conjugate transpose and with real eigenvalues. The energy observable is called the Hamiltonian.

Let $S$ denote the subsystem and $B$ its surroundings, conventionally called a bath. Their joint state space is the tensor product $\mathcal H_S\otimes\mathcal H_B$: a joint basis labels one system state and one bath state. The full Hamiltonian is
\begin{equation}
 H_{SB}=H_S\otimes I_B+I_S\otimes H_B+V,
 \qquad
 \rho_{SB}^{\rm eq}=\frac{e^{-\beta H_{SB}}}{Z_{SB}},
 \qquad
 Z_{SB}=\operatorname{Tr}_{SB}e^{-\beta H_{SB}}.
 \label{eq:open-composite}
\end{equation}
$H_S$ and $H_B$ are the uncoupled energy operators, $V$ is their interaction and $I$ is the identity on the indicated space. Here $\beta=1/(k_{\rm B}T_{\rm phys})$ is physical inverse temperature, with Boltzmann's constant $k_{\rm B}$. Assume $T_{\rm phys}>0$ and finite partition functions. The exponential weights joint energy eigenstates by their thermal probabilities. The density matrix $\rho_{SB}$ is a positive matrix of unit trace: its diagonal entries are probabilities in the chosen basis, while its off-diagonal entries encode phase coherence. The trace $\operatorname{Tr}$ sums diagonal entries.

The reduced state is $\rho_S=\operatorname{Tr}_B\rho_{SB}$. This \emph{partial trace} sums over an orthonormal bath basis while leaving a matrix on the system:
\begin{equation}
 (\rho_S)_{ij}=\sum_\mu
 \langle i,\mu|\rho_{SB}|j,\mu\rangle,
 \qquad
 \operatorname{Tr}_S(A\rho_S)
 =\operatorname{Tr}_{SB}[(A\otimes I_B)\rho_{SB}].
 \label{eq:partial-trace}
\end{equation}
The second identity explains why this is the right retained object: it reproduces every observable $A$ measured on $S$ alone. It does not retain every system--bath correlation. Distinct joint states can therefore have the same reduced state.

At equilibrium, the environmental influence can be kept in an effective energy operator. Define the uncoupled bath partition function $Z_B=\operatorname{Tr}_B e^{-\beta H_B}$ and
\begin{equation}
 R_S=\operatorname{Tr}_B e^{-\beta H_{SB}},
 \qquad
 H_{\rm MF}(\beta)=-\frac1\beta\log\frac{R_S}{Z_B},
 \qquad
 \rho_S^{\rm eq}=\frac{e^{-\beta H_{\rm MF}}}{Z_{\rm MF}},
 \quad Z_{\rm MF}=\frac{Z_{SB}}{Z_B}.
 \label{eq:mean-force-normalised}
\end{equation}
This is the \emph{Hamiltonian of mean force}. The logarithm acts on the positive reduced operator. Dividing by $Z_B$ fixes its scalar energy convention, while dividing by $Z_{\rm MF}$ normalises the system state. These are different operations: $R_S/Z_B$ is generally not a unit-trace density matrix. If $V=0$, the exponential factorises, the bath trace contributes $Z_B$, and $H_{\rm MF}=H_S$. With interaction, $H_{\rm MF}$ can depend on temperature and need not have the same eigenvectors or energy spacings as $H_S$. This is the operator counterpart of eliminating $\xi$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:effective-law}.~\cite{McCaulded509}

A small commuting example makes the change physical without requiring a bath approximation. Take two spins labelled $q,u=\pm1$, with joint energy $\epsilon q/2+\omega u/2+gqu$. The first spin is retained, the second is summed out. Its two possible surroundings contribute
\[
 R_S(q)=e^{-\beta\epsilon q/2}\,2\cosh[\beta(\omega/2+gq)],
 \qquad
 H_{\rm MF}(q)=\frac{\epsilon q}{2}
 -\frac1\beta\log\frac{\cosh[\beta(\omega/2+gq)]}{\cosh(\beta\omega/2)}.
\]
Here $\epsilon$ and $\omega$ set the uncoupled spin splittings and $g$ the interaction energy. Their units are energy. The effective splitting of the retained spin is
\[
 H_{\rm MF}(+1)-H_{\rm MF}(-1)
 =\epsilon-\frac1\beta\log
 \frac{\cosh[\beta(\omega/2+g)]}{\cosh[\beta(\omega/2-g)]}.
\]
The preference of the surrounding spin has changed the retained spin's thermal odds. In the symmetric case $\omega=0$, the interaction instead contributes the same scalar shift to both states. Which correlations matter therefore depends on symmetry, temperature and the observable as well as coupling strength.

The same boundary has a dynamical consequence. The joint density matrix evolves by $i\hbar\dot\rho_{SB}=[H_{SB},\rho_{SB}]$, where $\hbar$ is the reduced Planck constant and $[A,B]=AB-BA$. Taking the partial trace gives
\begin{equation}
 i\hbar\dot\rho_S=[H_S,\rho_S]
       +\operatorname{Tr}_B[V,\rho_{SB}].
 \label{eq:open-dynamical-residual}
\end{equation}
The second term still depends on the joint state. Two preparations with the same $\rho_S$ but different correlations can have different subsequent motion, just as the same region mass had two different escape rates in the four-state example. One can retain more environmental coordinates, make a justified closure approximation, or eliminate them into a history-dependent term of the kind in Eq.~\eqref{eq:general-memory}. The mean-force operator reproduces equilibrium statistics. It is not, by that fact alone, the generator of the reduced real-time motion.

This is the open-systems version of the representational question. An adequate description carries the influence that crosses its chosen boundary, not necessarily all the surrounding detail. The papers \href{/projection/pdf?corpus=equilibrium-from-the-inside-out&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fequilibrium-from-the-inside-out\%2Fmanuscript\%2Fimported\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3A04747c8164e7cb5c63d8cdcf867d5c9084d58985123d906cd5cead3037d6798f}{Equilibrium from the Inside Out (PDF)} and \href{/projection/pdf?corpus=dual-representatives-of-mean-force-equilibrium&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fdual-representatives-of-mean-force-equilibrium\%2Fmanuscript\%2Fimported\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3A3117c3353decba31edf180812ff4b0a5a0183d1c1b02ade147a1749c080dd47f}{Dual Representatives of Mean-Force Equilibrium (PDF)} pursue explicit ways of representing that influence.~\cite{McCaulbab685} The construction keeps the environmental influence in an object defined on the subsystem itself, making the choice of boundary part of the calculation.

\paragraph{Absorb the difficult coupling before approximating.}
The ground-state extension turns that representational choice into an analytical calculation. A spin strongly coupled to oscillators can be difficult to describe in the uncoupled basis because each spin orientation shifts the oscillators' preferred positions. Conditional displacement builds those shifts into the basis exactly. What remains is a bounded spin-flip operator. The bath spectrum then predicts the ground energy, the local spin state and its conditional surroundings, with independent diagonalisation of the original Hamiltonian providing the comparison in Fig.~\ref{fig:dd-hmf-physical-ground-state-20261016}.~\cite{McCaul2026e37796}

Consider the unbiased spin--boson Hamiltonian, in units with $\hbar=1$,
\begin{equation}
 H=H_B+g\tau_z B-\frac{\Delta}{2}\tau_x,
 \qquad H_B=\sum_k\nu_k b_k^\dagger b_k,
 \qquad B=\sum_kv_k(b_k+b_k^\dagger).
 \label{eq:hmf-physical-model}
\end{equation}
The two eigenvalues of $\tau_z$ label the spin orientations that exert opposite forces on the bath. The matrix $\tau_x$ flips them, with tunnelling energy $\Delta>0$. The operators $b_k^\dagger$ and $b_k$ add and remove one excitation of oscillator $k$, whose excitation energy is $\nu_k>0$. The real coefficient $v_k$ has units of energy and $g$ is a dimensionless coupling strength. We keep finitely many modes with gap $\nu_{\min}=\min_k\nu_k>0$ and expand in $\Delta<\nu_{\min}$, not in $g$.

Completing the oscillator squares shifts each mode by $d_k=gv_k/\nu_k$ and lowers the common energy by $g^2\lambda_0$, where $\lambda_0=\sum_kv_k^2/\nu_k$. The displacement operator is
$D(d)=\exp[\sum_kd_k(b_k^\dagger-b_k)]$. It changes coordinates without discarding the displaced bath states. Let $P_B=(-1)^{\sum_k b_k^\dagger b_k}$ distinguish even from odd total excitation number. In a sector of conserved combined spin and bath parity $\kappa=\pm1$, the joint eigenproblem becomes
\begin{equation}
 H_\kappa=H_B-g^2\lambda_0-\frac{\kappa\Delta}{2}T_g,
 \qquad T_g=D(2d)P_B,
 \qquad T_g^\dagger=T_g,\quad T_g^2=I.
 \label{eq:hmf-bounded-tunnelling}
\end{equation}
The last identity is the useful change: $\|T_g\|=1$ for every $g$. An interaction that was unbounded in oscillator coordinates has been absorbed into the representation, leaving a correction of size $\Delta/2$.

The retained spectral information is equally explicit. A spin flip exchanges the two displaced oscillator configurations. Its imaginary-time correlation, with $t$ measured in inverse energy, is
\begin{equation}
 C_g(t)=
 \exp\!\left[-4g^2\sum_k\frac{v_k^2}{\nu_k^2}
                 (1-e^{-\nu_k t})\right],
 \qquad
 S_g=\exp\!\left[-2g^2\sum_k\frac{v_k^2}{\nu_k^2}\right].
 \label{eq:hmf-retained-spectrum}
\end{equation}
$S_g$ is the overlap of the two displaced vacua. $C_g(t)$ retains the distribution of excitation energies deposited by a flip, rather than only their total energy shift. Define its excited-state inverse moments
\[
 A_g=\int_0^\infty[C_g(t)-S_g^2]\,dt,
 \qquad
 B_g=\int_0^\infty t[C_g(t)-S_g^2]\,dt.
\]
Subtracting $S_g^2$ removes the zero-excitation contribution. The remaining integrals weight virtual excitations by their inverse energies and inverse squared energies. On the positive-parity ground branch,
\begin{equation}
 E_0=-g^2\lambda_0-\frac{\Delta S_g}{2}
                      -\frac{\Delta^2 A_g}{4}+O(\Delta^3),
 \qquad
 \langle\tau_x\rangle_0=S_g+\Delta A_g+O(\Delta^2),
 \quad \langle\tau_z\rangle_0=0.
 \label{eq:hmf-ground-prediction}
\end{equation}
The first spin contribution comes from the overlap of the displaced vacua. The next comes from excursions through the excited bath. Both are calculated from the spectrum, without fitting the ground-state data. The fixed gap and bounded perturbation also give an explicit remainder for the displayed energy approximation $E_0^{(2)}$: for $\Delta<\varrho<\nu_{\min}$,
\[
 |E_0-E_0^{(2)}|
 \le \frac{\varrho}{2}
       \frac{(\Delta/\varrho)^3}{1-\Delta/\varrho}.
\]
Thus the change of representation makes the approximation controllable even when the physical bath coupling is not small.

The construction retains more than a local spin average. Write $Q_B=I-|0\rangle\langle0|$ for removal of the oscillator vacuum and
$|\eta_g\rangle=Q_BH_B^{-1}Q_BT_g|0\rangle$. The normalised bath trial state
$|\psi_{\rm tr}\rangle=(|0\rangle+\Delta|\eta_g\rangle/2)/\sqrt{1+\Delta^2B_g/4}$
reconstructs the physical joint state as
\begin{equation}
 |\Psi_{\rm tr}\rangle=
 \frac{|+\rangle D(-d)|\psi_{\rm tr}\rangle
       +|-\rangle D(d)P_B|\psi_{\rm tr}\rangle}{\sqrt2}.
 \label{eq:hmf-conditional-bath}
\end{equation}
Here $|\pm\rangle$ are the two $\tau_z$ orientations. This state can answer questions about the conditional bath as well as the spin, and its energy is a variational upper bound. The source comparison checks the joint vector separately from the reduced state.

