\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/portable-run-b69220b3a27348ad.result/dd-global-local-feedback.pdf}
\caption{The same optimum with different feedback. For the product-state example of Cerezo et al.~\cite{Cerezo2021232f10}, the global and local losses in Eq.~\eqref{eq:global-local-cost} have the same zero-cost target. With independent uniform angles, $\operatorname{Var}(\partial_j C_G)=\tfrac18(\tfrac38)^{n-1}$, while $\operatorname{Var}(\partial_j C_L)=1/(8n^2)$. Here $n$ is the number of qubits. Exact model variances isolate cost-dependent concentration.}
\label{fig:dd-global-local-feedback}
\end{figure}

Usable feedback needs more than a visible signal. Combining many responsive components can also make a correction too strong. Adaptive Normal Form and Phases of Hierarchy\footnote{\href{/projection/pdf?corpus=adaptive-normal-form-aek9&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fadaptive-normal-form\%2Fmanuscript\%2Ftex\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3Afb0134f7a5ab1a7f939c703b645c66f4460d074d0e3af9d63f2c1e25c9c0a4ad}{Adaptive Normal Form (PDF)} and \href{/references/source?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&key=local\%3A3517713b7efabf87b8e80ded&source_index=0&format=pdf}{The Phases of Hierarchy, developed v4 edition (PDF)}. Their one- and two-response-derivative protocols are kept distinct here.} study this stability problem. In the latter's sampled model, a plant state $x_k$ relaxes towards $s\theta_k$ between updates,
\[
 x_{k+1}=r x_k+(1-r)s\theta_k,\qquad
 e_k=x_{k+1}-b\theta_k,\qquad
 \theta_{k+1}=\theta_k+\alpha R_N(e_k).
\]
Here $r=e^{-h/\tau_r}$ is the fraction of the previous state retained over sampling interval $h$, $\tau_r$ the relaxation time, $s$ the plant gain, $b$ the reference gain, and $\alpha$ the update size. The response of a noisy majority of $N$ components is approximated by
$R_N(e)\simeq{\rm erf}(\beta e\sqrt{N/2})$, so its small-signal slope is $G_N\simeq\beta\sqrt{2N/\pi}$. Increasing $N$ increases the loop gain $K=\alpha G_N$.

For $0<r<1$ and $b>s>0$, the linearised discrete dynamics is stable only when
\begin{equation}
 0<K<
 \frac{2(1+r)}{(1+r)b-(1-r)s}.
 \label{eq:feedback-bound}
\end{equation}
In the declared example, $\alpha=0.35$, $\beta=0.6$, $b=1.2$, $s=0.4$ and $r=e^{-1}$ put the Gaussian-response crossing near $N=138.25$. Adding responsive components can therefore destabilise a loop whose individual parts have not changed. The boundary quantifies the permitted response gain in this sampled loop.~\cite{McCaul630942}

The hierarchy in Phases of Hierarchy makes that change explicit. Let layer $\ell$ combine $n_\ell$ inputs, so $L$ aggregation layers represent $N=\prod_{\ell=1}^L n_\ell$ bottom-level components. In the paper's iterated Gaussian approximation, the expected response obeys $m_\ell(e)=\operatorname{erf}(\sqrt{n_\ell/2}\,m_{\ell-1}(e))$, with individual slope $m_0'(0)=\beta$. Differentiating each stage at zero and multiplying its slopes gives
\begin{equation}
 K_N^{(L)}=\alpha\beta\sqrt{\frac{2N}{\pi}}\,c^{L-1},
 \qquad c=\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}},
 \qquad
 N_c^{(L)}=N_c^{(1)}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)^{L-1}.
 \label{eq:hierarchy-stability}
\end{equation}
Here $L=1$ is the flat aggregate. The factor $c$ is attenuation relative to a flat group of the same total size, not the absolute gain of each local block. One more aggregation layer therefore raises the population threshold by $\pi/2$ within this model. Figure~\ref{fig:dd-hierarchy-stability} shows the resulting phase diagram: a population of $200$ lies beyond the flat threshold but below the two-layer threshold at $\alpha=0.35$ and $h/\tau_r=1$. Slower updates provide another way to remain stable because the plant has longer to relax before the next correction.~\cite{McCaul630942}

Attenuating a response also has a cost. In the paper's separate depth-$L$ linear control cascade with attenuation $0<c<1$, the minimum input energy needed to produce a unit apex response scales as $c^{-2(L-1)}$, once the control horizon is long enough to reach the apex. The cascade provides a separate measure of the intervention cost of attenuation. Adaptive Normal Form supplies a related gradient-feedback construction, but its effective gain contains two response derivatives and scales differently.~\cite{McCaul74663e} The common design question is where an aggregate response should be represented and acted upon, with its stability, signal and control costs accounted for.\footnote{\href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=reading\%2Fadaptive-and-phases-interpretation-20261015.md&expected_sha256=7c66578b088ea00f4186e6e816b1156cfab4e332d4adc1b6096b9064790fe5ea&owner=writing-artifact}{Adaptive Normal Form and Phases of Hierarchy: exact editions, gain conventions, scaling models and figure sources}.}
