\end{figure}

\href{/projection/pdf?corpus=qde&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fqde\%2Fmanuscript\%2Ftex\%2Farxiv-2403.03350v2\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3Ad013235a42778633ccf7022db4a27b031b51c3143de4b0524f53cb7f362e21f8}{Quantum Dynamical Emulation of Imaginary Time Evolution (PDF)}, or ITQDE, provides a concrete measurement construction. It reconstructs filtered expectations from forward/backward overlaps using ancilla-assisted tests. The paper includes a two-system-qubit IBM Brisbane experiment with 104 random Clifford initial states and 4,000 samples per overlap, alongside separate numerical and simulator examples. This is evidence that the overlap route can be implemented on hardware at that scale. Its economy is reuse and reduced coherent-circuit requirements, paid for with sampling rather than free preparation of a filtered state.\footnote{J. M. Leamer, A. B. Magann, D. I. Bondar and G. McCaul, \emph{Quantum Dynamical Emulation of Imaginary Time Evolution}, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.03350}{arXiv:2403.03350}, version 2. \href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=reading\%2Fcontinuous-itqde-and-retrieval-comparisons-20261015.md&expected_sha256=963ef64435c0e555f4b7ed76785b232031ca88282490eaa94862b5f326900f2e&owner=writing-artifact}{Full primary-source interpretation and hardware/simulator distinction}.}~\cite{Leamer20249db941}

\href{/projection/pdf?corpus=free-snacks&file=synthetics+papers\%2Ffree-snacks\%2Fmanuscript\%2Ftex\%2Farxiv-2509.04618v1\%2Fv1.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3A93055a5490c67afd1731506d8325b688c0961c5f0dc8420858bd8ae331f044c7}{Free Snacks in Quantum Complexity, arXiv v1 (PDF)}~\cite{McCaul2025f7b569} focuses on when the ratio becomes unreliable. With estimated errors $\delta N,\delta Z$ and $|\delta Z|<Z$, elementary rearrangement gives
\[
 |\widehat F-F|\leq\frac{|\delta N|+|F|\,|\delta Z|}{Z-|\delta Z|}.
\]
A narrow window placed between widely separated supported levels has a small denominator. Even accurate overlap estimates can then give an unstable ratio. Within the paper's additive-noise model, resolving a very small gap while crossing a large one can demand a rapidly growing sample budget. The relevant bandwidth is a ratio of scales in the chosen spectral window, not simply the total number of qubits.

Local coarsening offers a controlled response. Convolving the numerator and denominator separately in probe energy with a normalised Gaussian of variance $\sigma^2$ gives a common prefactor $(1+2\tau\sigma^2)^{-1/2}$ and replaces $\tau$ by $\tau/(1+2\tau\sigma^2)$. The prefactor cancels in their ratio. This square-completion calculation uses separate numerator and denominator smoothing, which is generally different from smoothing their ratio. Existing overlap data can therefore answer a coarser question without rerunning a separate circuit for every probe energy. The price is merging distinctions. Whether that is acceptable depends on the observable or decision being sought.\footnote{G. McCaul, \emph{Free Snacks in Quantum Complexity}, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04618}{arXiv:2509.04618}, version 1. \href{/research/artifact?project=eigen-technical-due-diligence-briefing&scope=project&path=reading\%2Ffree-snacks-interpretation-20261015.md&expected_sha256=65a0416b0942ce36983422b9c82da575cc905bce71d8144f32c20b12a4f0ee19&owner=writing-artifact}{Filter derivation, resource distinctions and exact edition corrections}.}

Together, these prototypes supply a practical programme. Preserve the operator information that a scalar reading hides. Add omitted directions according to their expected value, not merely their magnitude. Choose spectral resolution where the estimator can support it. Candidate preparation, measurements, optimisation and conditioning then belong in one cost comparison. The same design problem appears when the large object is a collection of records rather than a quantum state: retain what a future query will need, and provide a way to recover what the smaller description omits.

\section{Memory as a retained and reconstructable object}
\label{section:memory-as-a-retained-and-reconstructable-object}

A memory is a representation built now to answer questions later. An early implementation of this idea is developed in Memory in Pieces\footnote{\href{/projection/pdf?corpus=memory-in-pieces&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fcorpus-as-a-state\%2Freconstruction\%2Foutput\%2Fpdf\%2Fv2.1\%2Fskein.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3Adbc3b568b21a1f3c6d6b7ddff85b3e5ac34b9753052a8ecf1e361f72b0cbb009}{Memory in Pieces (PDF)}, version 2.1. Its main sections on retained objects, repeated grading and exact continuation, together with the news-construction appendix, supply the steps developed here. \href{/projection/pdf?corpus=corpus-as-a-state&file=synthetics+papers\%2Fcorpus-as-a-state\%2Fmanuscript\%2Fmain.pdf&expected_content_revision=sha256\%3Aedea48ee1e9ade2a1b5ea9b39ae8b08c476eb5db2c575cb99d045e8563a5499b}{Corpus as a State (PDF)} is the earlier geometric construction.} and its predecessor Corpus as a State. Together they turned retained structure, reconstruction and closure residuals into a working classical memory: source items were ingested into local descriptions, selected task directions were retained, and queries were answered from those descriptions or returned to finer source records. The implementation was developed under the names Skein and later Parallax. Its campaigns tested both exact relational queries and language-based retrieval. They achieved a substantial warm-query advantage on a supplied composable relation, useful source-grounded answers in text collections, and an unusually informative diagnosis of where those answers fail.

The distinction between those outcomes is the point of the example. A small retained rule can answer joint questions that independent summaries cannot. But an embedding of a question does not automatically discover or execute that rule. The language tests reveal headroom precisely at this interface: how ingestion records entities, roles, events and time, and how a question becomes an operation on them. We first develop the retained object, then examine what the implementations actually managed.

The original \emph{Corpus as a State}~\cite{Referencece8431} begins with vectors describing source items. If the rows of $X$ are normalised item vectors, its state-like summary is
\begin{equation}
 \rho=\frac{X^T X}{N},\qquad q^T\rho q=\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N(x_i^Tq)^2.
 \label{eq:corpus-state}
\end{equation}
Here $N$ is the number of items and $q$ a unit query direction. The quadratic form reports how much of the collection lies along that direction. It does not by itself identify which document supplied the contribution. Source identities and witness records must remain available separately. The quantum-like notation packages familiar covariance and projection geometry into an account of what the collection retains.

For reconstruction, the mean and centred covariance matter. A node can retain a mean $\mu$, an orthonormal local basis $V$ and coordinates along that basis. The discarded covariance eigenvalues quantify mean-square embedding reconstruction error. Parent nodes combine child mass, means and covariance, including both the variation within children and the variation between their means. If a child has already discarded a direction, its error must be carried upwards. Recomputing a small parent residual from truncated children does not recover the lost information.

The original work also gives a certified search rule. Suppose every item in a node has the form $x=\mu+Va+e$, with $e$ orthogonal to $V$, $\|a\|\leq R$ and $\|e\|\leq r$. Then its signed query score satisfies
\begin{equation}
 q^Tx\leq q^T\mu+R\|V^Tq\|+r\|(I-VV^T)q\|.
 \label{eq:search-bound}
\end{equation}
A best-first search may discard a node once this upper bound cannot beat an already found answer. That certificate is different from routing by the nearest centroid. The latter may be fast, but without a bound it is a heuristic. A hierarchy also need not save work: loose bounds can force it to open most nodes, in which case a direct vector scan is preferable.

\paragraph{Building the operator from source records.}
The Parallax construction begins with individually identified records and a declared set of questions, not with an operator whose meaning is left to be inferred. In the news example there are 609 articles. A language model reads each article alone and extracts four short fields: object, mechanism, conditions and consequences. Each field has at most thirty words and supporting quotations checked against the article. A frozen encoder maps the fields to 384-component vectors. These descriptions choose the semantic coordinates. Everything that follows is a specified numerical construction on those observations.~\cite{McCaul2026164581}

Consider one field, and write $v_i$ for the unit vector of article $i$. Similarity proposes which records should exchange probability in an auxiliary diffusion. For each article, keep its sixteen nearest neighbours and give a directed edge the nonnegative weight
\[
 h(v_i^Tv_j)=\left(\frac{1+v_i^Tv_j}{2}\right)^2.
\]
Take $a_{ij}=a_{ji}$ to be the larger of the two directed weights, using zero for an absent edge. A record missing this field remains identified but has its incident edges removed. With reference weights $\pi_i=1/609$, define
\begin{equation}
 r_j=\sum_{i\ne j}\frac{a_{ij}}{\pi_j},\qquad
 \kappa=\max\{1,\max_j r_j\},\qquad
 G_{ij}=\frac{a_{ij}}{\kappa\pi_j}\ (i\ne j),\quad
 G_{jj}=-\frac{r_j}{\kappa}.
 \label{eq:memory-generator-construction}
\end{equation}
Column $j$ gives rates out of source $j$. Its entries sum to zero, so total probability is preserved. The symmetric conductance $G_{ij}\pi_j=a_{ij}/\kappa$ gives detailed balance, and the largest exit rate is at most one. This last normalisation fixes the diffusion time used in the tests. In a physical application $G$ can instead be supplied by the actual dynamics. Here it describes a deliberately constructed similarity process on records.

The next step makes this process amenable to spectral compression. Let $p$ be a probability distribution over source IDs, let $W=\mathrm{diag}(\pi)$, and express departures from the reference in weighted coordinates:
\begin{equation}
 y=W^{-1/2}(p-\pi),\qquad L=W^{-1/2}GW^{1/2},\qquad
 A=U^TLU,\quad D=U^TL(I-UU^T).
 \label{eq:memory-object}
\end{equation}
The first transformation gives $\|y\|^2=\sum_i(p_i-\pi_i)^2/\pi_i$. Detailed balance makes $L$ symmetric and nonpositive. Its negative, $H_{\rm mem}=-L$, is the positive dissipation operator: it scores variation across the source graph. The columns of $U$ will specify the retained directions. Once they have been selected, $A$ is the smaller operator acting on retained coordinates, while $D$ measures feedback from the omitted ones.

\paragraph{Choosing what a local memory must retain.}
A candidate group of records needs both its total mass and the distinctions within it that a query can resolve. Let $C_{ai}=1$ when source $i$ belongs to proposed group $a$, and zero otherwise. The group masses are $\pi_c=C\pi$. The normalised constant direction for each group is a column of
\[
 U_0=W^{1/2}C^T\mathrm{diag}(\pi_c)^{-1/2},\qquad P_0=U_0U_0^T .
\]
These columns preserve probability assigned to each group. They do not preserve, for example, which of two otherwise similar articles records a purchase before rather than after an approval.

A task vector $f$ assigns a numerical value to each source. A date/source selection can be an indicator, while a field-direction test can be the projection of that article's embedding onto a specified query direction. Its weighted column is $W^{1/2}f$. In the news run, the declared field-direction ensemble gives half its weight to observed source directions and half to differences between each source and its nearest neighbour. These contrast probes make local distinctions part of the demand, rather than asking only for the mean field. Inside each proposed group, remove the constant part and collect the remaining task columns together with columns of $(I-P_0)LU_0$. The latter identify internal variations coupled to the proposed group dynamics. After applying the declared task and dynamics weights, call this local demand matrix $E$.

The leading left singular vectors of $E$, equivalently the leading eigenvectors of $EE^T$, give the rank-limited internal directions $B$. They maximise the retained squared demand at the chosen rank. The resulting map is $U=[U_0,B]$, with orthonormal columns and each internal direction supported within its group. This is a direct linear-algebra calculation, not a further language-model training stage. The news implementation gives task and dynamical demands equal family weights, scales the dynamical demand by $0.1$, and permits at most eight internal coordinates per parent. It then checks the actual task losses and the full closure defect. A good spectral approximation proposes a useful memory, while these checks determine whether it is adequate.~\cite{Referencea10bf0,McCaul2026164581}

The groups themselves are proposals too. Diffusion eigenvectors provide coordinates in which a reproducible clustering routine suggests neighbouring records to combine. The construction accepts a new level only if it reduces the object count and meets its declared error thresholds. At the next level, all retained child coordinates enter the parent calculation, including the internal contrasts. The parent carries the transformed operator, the decoder, exact source membership and the inherited error account. Passing only child averages would throw away the very information the internal coordinates were purchased to retain.

With $z=U^TW^{-1/2}(p-\pi)$, the linear reconstruction and task estimate are
\[
 \widehat p=\pi+W^{1/2}Uz,\qquad
 \widehat{f^Tp}=f^T\pi+f^TW^{1/2}Uz.
\]
This reconstructs linear task values. Positivity of every reconstructed probability is a separate constraint.

For a four-record illustration, suppose the source cards explicitly mark record 1 as a purchase before approval and record 2 as a purchase after approval. Put those records in one group and records 3 and 4 in another, with uniform reference $\pi_i=1/4$. The query \emph{before approval} has task vector $f=(1,0,0,0)^T$. Keeping group mass alone maps either source selection $p=(1,0,0,0)^T$ or $p=(0,1,0,0)^T$ to the same reconstruction $\hat p=(1/2,1/2,0,0)^T$, hence the same estimated answer $f^T\hat p=1/2$. Adding the internal contrast $b=(1,-1,0,0)^T/\sqrt2$ to $U$ recovers the exact answers one and zero. The illustration shows what the selected direction buys: the decoder can use a distinction that the source cards and task definition made explicit. If ingestion never records that temporal distinction, choosing a larger numerical basis alone cannot supply its meaning.


For the declared task vector $f$, the desired expectation is $f^Tp$. Writing $P_U=UU^T$, the error from discarding the orthogonal fluctuation is bounded by
\begin{equation}
 |\delta(f^Tp)|\leq\|(I-P_U)W^{1/2}f\|\sqrt{\chi^2(p\Vert\pi)}.
 \label{eq:memory-task-error}
\end{equation}
The first factor measures whether the task direction was retained. The second uses the chi-squared divergence $\chi^2(p\Vert\pi)=\sum_i(p_i-\pi_i)^2/\pi_i$ to measure how far the queried distribution moves from the reference. A basis that works near the average collection may be inadequate for a rare conditional slice. This gives refinement a concrete trigger. Under reversible contraction, the corresponding dynamical mismatch has a finite-time bound proportional to $t\|D\|$, and exact elimination again supplies a memory kernel when an autonomous approximation is inadequate.


Figure~\ref{fig:dd-memory-mechanism} follows both versions from source items to an answer. In the original route, local geometric summaries support projection and certified search. In the extension, retained task/law coordinates and reconstruction travel together. A query selects a task or source-conditioned distribution, the decoder computes the requested quantity, and a residual or capacity failure can send the calculation back to finer records. A language model may compose a final response from returned witnesses, but its prose is not the same object as the numerical decoder.
